    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e6c20001-174f-50de-b090-0602c765414a",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $6x-1=5x-4$\n- **II.** $x-6=2x+5$\n- **III.** $6x+9=6x+9$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-1 & =5x-4 \\\\ 6x-1-5x & =5x-4-5x \\\\ x-1 & =-4 \\\\ x-1+1 & =-4+1 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $6x-1=5x-4$ has the unique solution $x=-3.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-6 & =2x+5 \\\\ x-6-x & =2x+5-x \\\\ -6 & =x+5 \\\\ -6-5 & =x+5-5 \\\\ x & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $x-6=2x+5$ has the unique solution $x=-11.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+9 & =6x+9 \\\\ 6x+9-6x & =6x+9-6x \\\\ 9 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $6x+9=6x+9$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
