    "question": {
      ":question/id": "69640dd9-755b-5cbf-99a3-4adbd76f71b0",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $5x+3=4x-5$\n- **II.** $-7x+1=-7x+1$\n- **III.** $-5x+3=-5x-8$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+3 & =4x-5 \\\\ 5x+3-4x & =4x-5-4x \\\\ x+3 & =-5 \\\\ x+3-3 & =-5-3 \\\\ x & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $5x+3=4x-5$ has the unique solution $x=-8.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x+1 & =-7x+1 \\\\ -7x+1+7x & =-7x+1+7x \\\\ 1 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-7x+1=-7x+1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x+3 & =-5x-8 \\\\ -5x+3+5x & =-5x-8+5x \\\\ 3 & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
