    "question": {
      ":question/id": "066978f4-7126-5eb0-a44b-4ea8617a1ad4",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-x-5=-x+6$\n- **II.** $x-5=x-4$\n- **III.** $x-1=2x-1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-5 & =-x+6 \\\\ -x-5+x & =-x+6+x \\\\ -5 & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5 & =x-4 \\\\ x-5-x & =x-4-x \\\\ -5 & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =2x-1 \\\\ x-1-x & =2x-1-x \\\\ -1 & =x-1 \\\\ -1+1 & =x-1+1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $x-1=2x-1$ has the unique solution $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
