    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3b983876-7c66-5e46-b9d7-73b274af8602",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2x+1=2x+5$\n- **II.** $-7x+2=-7x$\n- **III.** $6x+9=7x+6$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =2x+5 \\\\ 2x+1-2x & =2x+5-2x \\\\ 1 & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x+2 & =-7x \\\\ -7x+2+7x & =-7x+7x \\\\ 2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+9 & =7x+6 \\\\ 6x+9-6x & =7x+6-6x \\\\ 9 & =x+6 \\\\ 9-6 & =x+6-6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $6x+9=7x+6$ has the unique solution $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
