    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3f30d54b-f1eb-5628-bbe4-fd97607340f6",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2x+1=x-4$\n- **II.** $3x+8=2x-7$\n- **III.** $-x-1=-x-1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =x-4 \\\\ 2x+1-x & =x-4-x \\\\ x+1 & =-4 \\\\ x+1-1 & =-4-1 \\\\ x & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x+1=x-4$ has the unique solution $x=-5.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+8 & =2x-7 \\\\ 3x+8-2x & =2x-7-2x \\\\ x+8 & =-7 \\\\ x+8-8 & =-7-8 \\\\ x & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3x+8=2x-7$ has the unique solution $x=-15.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-1 & =-x-1 \\\\ -x-1+x & =-x-1+x \\\\ -1 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-x-1=-x-1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
